WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.753 --> 00:00:04.963
Whoa!

00:00:06.047 --> 00:00:07.716
Before a trailer starts on YouTube

00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:08.925
they call them bumpers.

00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:11.720
Because if a trailer just started 
the way, you know

00:00:11.761 --> 00:00:13.471
it does right after the green card

00:00:13.471 --> 00:00:16.516
where we kind of get into the story
and we meet our characters...

00:00:16.599 --> 00:00:17.934
people might turn it off.

00:00:17.934 --> 00:00:19.436
But if you've been given a taste 

00:00:19.436 --> 00:00:21.730
oh, it's going to get crazy by the end!

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:23.982
So that's the thinking behind that.

00:00:25.066 --> 00:00:27.485
So I'm Bill Neil and I'm a trailer editor

00:00:27.485 --> 00:00:29.446
and I work at Buddha Jones.

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:36.703
That is a project where we 
worked from dailies...

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.165
where we didn't get a cut of the movie at first.

00:00:40.165 --> 00:00:42.500
And so I just watched dailies

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.085
as they came in, as they were shot.

00:00:44.085 --> 00:00:45.587
And you start to get kind of a sense

00:00:45.587 --> 00:00:46.963
of what the movie's going to be

00:00:46.963 --> 00:00:48.923
and you start to kind of get a feel.

00:00:51.843 --> 00:00:53.595
"But that's why back at 
the Haywood Ranch..."

00:00:53.595 --> 00:00:55.930
The song is Fingertips by Stevie Wonder.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:58.141
Jordan Peele suggested 
to use that song.

00:00:58.224 --> 00:01:01.186
You know, we're playing it 
almost diagetically at first.

00:01:01.186 --> 00:01:02.145
"We had skin in the game."

00:01:02.145 --> 00:01:04.064
Like diagetic means, like 
it's part of the scene

00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:05.106
and then she's dancing to it

00:01:05.106 --> 00:01:08.401
and then do a little kind of fun
editorial tricks

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:10.278
where like, on the beat of the music

00:01:10.278 --> 00:01:11.654
you try to have his hoofs...

00:01:11.946 --> 00:01:13.823
kind of hit the beats.

00:01:15.116 --> 00:01:18.912
And then we cut outside
to Daniel with the horse

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:21.039
and making the music 
really kind of echoey

00:01:21.039 --> 00:01:22.749
and it's kind of creepy.

00:01:23.708 --> 00:01:26.878
And then go to that super quiet, 
big, wide shot.

00:01:27.378 --> 00:01:30.006
The other thing I do there, 
I have the crickets...

00:01:30.715 --> 00:01:33.176
and then I have the crickets

00:01:33.176 --> 00:01:35.261
stop chirping and it just goes quiet.

00:01:35.678 --> 00:01:36.513
I'm taking a cue

00:01:36.513 --> 00:01:38.473
from the movie The Fog:

00:01:38.473 --> 00:01:40.225
John Carpenter's The Fog.

00:01:40.225 --> 00:01:41.434
There's a moment in the fog

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:44.646
where right before the evil fog rolls in 
there's crickets

00:01:44.646 --> 00:01:47.649
and then it's a wide shot of the house
and they stop chirping.

00:01:47.649 --> 00:01:50.860
And then the fog kind of rolls in.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.196
So I kind of took it from that.

00:01:53.696 --> 00:01:54.906
It's an homage, I guess.

00:01:54.906 --> 00:01:56.157
Or I'm stealing. I'm not sure.

00:01:56.157 --> 00:01:58.201
And then after that,
we have the big

00:01:58.201 --> 00:02:01.412
"From Jordan Peele"
kind of come down.

00:02:01.746 --> 00:02:04.541
And I guess it's known now that

00:02:04.541 --> 00:02:06.668
it's kind of Jordan Peele's
alien movie

00:02:06.668 --> 00:02:07.877
but at the time

00:02:07.877 --> 00:02:10.421
wasn't for sure what the heck
was going on.

00:02:10.421 --> 00:02:13.424
And I thought the big thing 
coming down...

00:02:13.842 --> 00:02:16.761
"From" underneath the "Jordan Peele"

00:02:16.761 --> 00:02:19.222
I felt—because normally "from" 
would be on top

00:02:19.222 --> 00:02:20.640
of the name Jordan Peele.

00:02:20.849 --> 00:02:22.016
But...

00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:25.270
I flipped it because it 
reminded me of the way

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:27.897
the spaceship looked in 
"Close Encounters of the Third Kind"

00:02:27.897 --> 00:02:28.731
when it came down.

00:02:28.731 --> 00:02:31.693
And also that shot of that house

00:02:31.693 --> 00:02:33.778
reminded me of the shot from 
"Close Encounters"

00:02:33.778 --> 00:02:35.780
when the little kid gets abducted.

00:02:38.241 --> 00:02:41.578
We start deconstructing 
the song of "Fingertips"

00:02:41.578 --> 00:02:45.915
and just using the rhythm 
from it.

00:02:46.249 --> 00:02:49.127
If you ever heard that song, 
this is very deconstructed.

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:55.425
And then we add a lot of sound design 
and layers and rises...

00:02:55.717 --> 00:02:58.386
and stuff underneath.

00:02:59.137 --> 00:03:01.222
A rise is—it's a piece of music.
It's—

00:03:01.222 --> 00:03:03.016
[mimics tone going up
and also getting louder]

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:04.517
and kind of getting bigger and bigger
and bigger

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:05.935
and louder and louder 
and louder.

00:03:05.935 --> 00:03:07.729
[tone going up and 
getting louder]

00:03:07.729 --> 00:03:10.315
In the early days, when 
I was cutting trailers

00:03:10.315 --> 00:03:14.861
there are basically no 
trailer sound design libraries

00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:16.863
and I'd want something like a rise 
and I'd be like

00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:18.573
"I want something that sounds like

00:03:18.573 --> 00:03:20.200
that Beatles song, 
A Day in the Life."

00:03:21.784 --> 00:03:24.495
Because at the end of that, 
it has a big, huge rise.

00:03:24.579 --> 00:03:28.208
Now there's about a billion and a billion
differnt kinds of like

00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:29.209
choirs...

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:30.752
and synths...

00:03:30.835 --> 00:03:34.797
and huge orchestras and hits and 
everything you can imagine.

00:03:34.881 --> 00:03:37.467
Not even just music, like just 
sound effects libraries.

00:03:37.467 --> 00:03:39.427
There's like, like a little kid laughing.

00:03:39.427 --> 00:03:41.512
And I've used this sound effect a lot.

00:03:41.512 --> 00:03:44.474
[Ominous, piano tone
overlayed by child's laughter]

00:03:51.564 --> 00:03:56.194
[explosion sounds and 
crackling]

00:03:56.194 --> 00:03:59.155
[percussive marimba tones
and timpani]

00:03:59.656 --> 00:04:00.323
"Attack!"

00:04:00.323 --> 00:04:00.990
"Russia."

00:04:01.324 --> 00:04:04.285
[percussive marimba tones
and timpani]

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:07.705
"Oh."

00:04:07.705 --> 00:04:08.915
Stanley Kubrick had, like

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:10.750
some of the best trailers of all time.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:12.460
That's so innovative.

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:13.294
I mean...

00:04:13.962 --> 00:04:16.589
I've always looked at that
as one of those crazy

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:18.675
you know, kind of groundbreaking trailers.

00:04:18.675 --> 00:04:20.677
That's back in the 60s when, like

00:04:20.677 --> 00:04:23.888
the French new wave was starting
to have an effect on Hollywood.

00:04:23.888 --> 00:04:27.517
And that was embracing that crazy,
non-linear, weird editing.

00:04:27.517 --> 00:04:29.644
Almost overboard.

00:04:29.644 --> 00:04:30.895
Back in the 60s, they didn't have

00:04:30.895 --> 00:04:32.438
trailer houses really doing it.

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:34.190
I think there were people 
on the lot doing that

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:36.025
or the filmmakers were doing it.

00:04:36.025 --> 00:04:38.319
"You have a date with Carrie."

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:44.075
Carrie is...

00:04:45.034 --> 00:04:46.452
it's a lot of VO.

00:04:46.661 --> 00:04:48.913
"A girl who lives in that creepy house."

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:52.125
The thing about the Carrie trailer

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:54.544
that I always say is that it kind of gives away...

00:04:54.919 --> 00:04:57.463
every big moment in the movie.

00:04:57.714 --> 00:05:00.842
"For Carrie, it will be a dream come true."

00:05:00.842 --> 00:05:04.178
"For everyone else, it will be 
a nightmare."

00:05:04.178 --> 00:05:05.471
You see who dies.

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:07.557
You see what happens to her at the prom.

00:05:07.557 --> 00:05:08.850
You kind of see everything.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.727
So when people complain about trailers

00:05:10.727 --> 00:05:12.729
giving away everything nowadays...

00:05:13.146 --> 00:05:15.231
I mean, like, well look at 
the "Carrie" trailer.

00:05:15.231 --> 00:05:17.442
The other fun thing about 
the "Carrie" trailer

00:05:17.442 --> 00:05:21.529
is if you see like—
there's a part in it

00:05:21.529 --> 00:05:25.658
where they have a wipe in the frame...

00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:29.370
when the blood is poured on her
and it wipes the frame red.

00:05:29.537 --> 00:05:30.788
And although in the movie

00:05:30.788 --> 00:05:32.832
it does kind of—the lights 
all go red

00:05:32.832 --> 00:05:34.584
they kind of go red after that.

00:05:34.584 --> 00:05:37.670
But I think they had 
to do that because

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:40.548
the MPAA has to approve all trailers

00:05:40.548 --> 00:05:42.300
for general audience viewing.

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:45.053
Doesn't allow you to show 
actual red blood.

00:05:45.053 --> 00:05:47.722
You can't, you know, for a regular 
green band trailer

00:05:47.722 --> 00:05:51.809
which is meant for all audiences, 
you can't show blood.

00:05:54.771 --> 00:05:57.231
That is just one of 
the best trailers ever.

00:05:57.523 --> 00:05:58.483
It's so good!

00:05:58.483 --> 00:06:01.444
That sound design is amazing.

00:06:01.444 --> 00:06:04.405
[high pitched rising tone
increasing in intensity]

00:06:05.865 --> 00:06:08.368
That alien [vocalizes a howl].

00:06:08.368 --> 00:06:09.911
[high pitched tone continues]

00:06:09.911 --> 00:06:10.870
That screaming sound.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.498
Nowadays, we kind of call that like 
a signature sound

00:06:13.498 --> 00:06:16.292
which is kind of a repeated sound that you kind of

00:06:16.292 --> 00:06:18.294
you know, kind of latch on to.

00:06:18.294 --> 00:06:20.671
I sort of really kind of focusing 
on signature sounds

00:06:20.671 --> 00:06:23.966
like with the trailer I did for 
"Texas Chainsaw Massacre".

00:06:23.966 --> 00:06:26.803
The sound I'm talking about 
is this this crazy camera—

00:06:26.803 --> 00:06:28.262
[vocalizing, imitating the high-pitched 
sound a camera's flash makes]

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:31.057
[high pitched, mechanical whine]

00:06:33.017 --> 00:06:35.395
And I loved the original

00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:39.190
and I wanted to kind of 
make reference to that.

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:41.359
"This film is positively ruthless

00:06:41.359 --> 00:06:44.237
in its attempt to drive you 
right out of your mind."

00:06:44.237 --> 00:06:45.279
[high pitched, mechanical whine]

00:06:45.279 --> 00:06:46.906
So, when I was a kid

00:06:46.906 --> 00:06:51.077
I loved, like, those horror—
viny records of sound effects.

00:06:51.077 --> 00:06:52.412
Like "Sounds to Make You Shiver".

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:56.165
[evil, witch-like laughter]

00:06:56.749 --> 00:06:58.835
Disney's like, 
"Haunted Mansion".

00:06:58.835 --> 00:07:01.129
[thunder and rain effect]

00:07:01.129 --> 00:07:04.424
And so I guess maybe that kind of 
inspired me a little bit

00:07:04.424 --> 00:07:07.301
you know, in terms of being too focused
on the sounds of horror.

00:07:11.139 --> 00:07:13.516
"When the world is in trouble..."

00:07:13.516 --> 00:07:15.977
In the 90s, there was a lot of VO [voice over]

00:07:15.977 --> 00:07:18.187
and a little bit more of a formula.

00:07:18.187 --> 00:07:20.898
"The most brilliant commander 
in the Soviet navy..."

00:07:21.065 --> 00:07:22.984
"From the sewers of Gotham..."

00:07:22.984 --> 00:07:25.445
I mean, it's a cliché to say 
"In a world...".

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:29.282
"In a world where both of our cars 
were totally underwater..."

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:31.492
"and advanced technology..."

00:07:31.492 --> 00:07:34.370
"1500 active volcanoes in the world..."

00:07:34.745 --> 00:07:37.039
We worked with Don Lafontaine

00:07:37.039 --> 00:07:39.750
who was the the King of VO.

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:42.378
He's the one you always associate 
with "In a world...".

00:07:42.378 --> 00:07:43.838
He was really cool.

00:07:43.838 --> 00:07:47.508
He was always professional, but he 
was like the number one.

00:07:47.508 --> 00:07:49.302
And he had his own limo.

00:07:49.302 --> 00:07:52.930
And he would be driven from 
VO booth to VO booth.

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:55.725
There are people who try to
break into the business

00:07:55.725 --> 00:07:57.518
and then you'd give them a shot.

00:07:57.518 --> 00:08:00.188
And there was such a difference between...

00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:04.442
you know, a Don Lafontaine 
who would nail it.

00:08:05.568 --> 00:08:08.237
"They destroyed everything he had..."

00:08:08.237 --> 00:08:11.866
He would nail whatever kind of 
nuance you that you wanted

00:08:11.866 --> 00:08:13.868
and somebody who just didn't know 
how to do it—

00:08:13.868 --> 00:08:16.078
And it seems like it's easy, 
but it was really hard.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:23.211
You have such a short time 
in any trailer to kind of...

00:08:24.253 --> 00:08:26.672
engage people or get people 
to lock in.

00:08:26.672 --> 00:08:30.426
And I think sound and music 
and sound effects...

00:08:31.093 --> 00:08:32.845
they're the soul of the trailer.

00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:34.931
There's another part in that trailer 
that uses sound that—

00:08:34.931 --> 00:08:36.641
where we go to black.

00:08:36.641 --> 00:08:39.936
We stop everything, 
we go to black.

00:08:39.936 --> 00:08:41.896
And we do have a couple cards

00:08:41.896 --> 00:08:43.731
that say, like "From producer 
Michael Bay"

00:08:43.731 --> 00:08:46.817
but we have the sound of this girl
running and screaming

00:08:46.817 --> 00:08:47.485
and it's in the black.

00:08:47.485 --> 00:08:49.695
And you hear these these footsteps...

00:08:49.695 --> 00:08:51.989
[heavy footsteps and the sound 
of breathing]

00:08:51.989 --> 00:08:53.699
And then it's quiet and then—

00:08:53.699 --> 00:08:54.951
[chainsaw motor starting]

00:08:54.951 --> 00:08:55.993
chainsaw comes through.

00:08:55.993 --> 00:08:58.371
And that's another one of 
my favorite parts of it.

00:09:02.208 --> 00:09:04.335
So sometimes we get 
a rough cut of the movie.

00:09:06.128 --> 00:09:08.172
50% of the time we don't even 
get a rough cut.

00:09:08.172 --> 00:09:10.967
We get to start getting dailies
as soon as they start shooting.

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:15.263
We're rarely, if ever, working from
a final cut of a feature.

00:09:15.263 --> 00:09:17.807
And "Halloween" I would say...

00:09:17.807 --> 00:09:19.684
the line reading Jamie has of...

00:09:19.934 --> 00:09:20.893
"So I could kill him."

00:09:21.269 --> 00:09:22.228
"So I can kill him."

00:09:22.770 --> 00:09:24.855
...in the middle of the trailer 
is a different take.

00:09:26.023 --> 00:09:27.358
"So I can kill him."

00:09:27.358 --> 00:09:29.902
And then I think we edited the

00:09:29.902 --> 00:09:31.737
the end scare of that trailer

00:09:31.737 --> 00:09:33.072
 where the the girl...

00:09:34.448 --> 00:09:37.159
is trying to close the closet door 
and then Michael appears.

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:40.955
That is edited differently than 
it is in the final movie.

00:09:40.955 --> 00:09:42.498
And I remember I saw it and I was like

00:09:42.498 --> 00:09:44.875
"Oh, they cut that differently than 
I thought they would."

00:09:48.296 --> 00:09:51.340
Well, the beginning of the 
"10 Cloverfield Lane" trailer...

00:09:51.882 --> 00:09:54.051
is, you know, kind of just 
a fun, goofy song.

00:09:54.051 --> 00:09:55.219
They seem like they're 
having fun.

00:09:55.219 --> 00:09:56.929
It's not really like 
jokey together.

00:09:58.139 --> 00:10:00.016
They've called it a rug pull

00:10:00.016 --> 00:10:03.185
where it starts off one way 
and then it changes.

00:10:05.646 --> 00:10:08.649
The rug pull in that is a little bit 
more gradual.

00:10:08.649 --> 00:10:12.069
A normal rug pull is, you know, 
"Oh my God, it looks funny."

00:10:12.069 --> 00:10:13.904
It's funny. And then bam, 
something changes.

00:10:13.904 --> 00:10:15.698
"I can talk to Santa."

00:10:15.698 --> 00:10:17.908
"All right, revelers."

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:20.369
"Welcome to your worst 
Christmas ever."

00:10:20.369 --> 00:10:21.912
"Let's go!"

00:10:26.626 --> 00:10:29.920
Using sound effects or 
movements, punches...

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:32.965
in beat with the music and 
time with the music.

00:10:32.965 --> 00:10:34.258
It's all over the place.

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:38.679
Rhythm is such like a basic human sense...

00:10:39.096 --> 00:10:42.099
that when you kind of tap into that 
in such a kind of cool way

00:10:42.099 --> 00:10:43.309
way with the punches, I mean

00:10:43.309 --> 00:10:46.771
I use a little bit in "Once Upon a Time
in Hollywood".

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:49.732
I call it "click click 
whir whir".

00:10:51.734 --> 00:10:53.069
Because it's kind of 
what it is.

00:10:53.069 --> 00:10:56.656
It's this kind of use of rhythm 
and rhythmic cutting.

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:03.037
One metaphor I want to say about trailers.

00:11:03.037 --> 00:11:06.457
This was told to me by a studio exec 
named Tony Sella.

00:11:06.457 --> 00:11:08.626
So this metaphor about trailers is like.

00:11:08.626 --> 00:11:11.921
It's like trying to sell a jigsaw puzzle.

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:14.715
And in order to sell the jigsaw puzzle

00:11:14.715 --> 00:11:16.717
you can't show the whole picture 
on the box.

00:11:16.717 --> 00:11:20.471
You are only allowed to show four pieces 
of that puzzle.

